*help.txt*	For Vim version 9.2 on the @CHIP@.  Last change: 2026 Sep 25

		   VIM on @CHIP@ - main help file
									 k
      Move around:  Use the cursor keys, or "h" to go left,	       h   l
		    "j" to go down, "k" to go up, "l" to go right.	 j
Close this window:  Use ":q<Enter>".
   Get out of Vim:  Use ":qa!<Enter>" (careful, all changes are lost!).
		    Vim is the whole device, so it then offers a fresh
		    session rather than leaving you nowhere.  |esp-quit|

Jump to a subject:  Position the cursor on a tag (e.g. |bars|) and hit CTRL-].
   With the mouse:  Double-click the left mouse button on a tag, e.g. |bars|.
		    The mouse is already enabled.
	Jump back:  Type CTRL-O.  Repeat to go further back.

This is Vim 9.2 running directly on an @CHIP@ microcontroller.  It is real
Vim: the same editor, the same commands, Vim script, syntax highlighting,
windows, buffers, registers, macros, :vimgrep, diffs.  What is different is
the machine around it, and that is what this file is about.

Vim's full documentation is 12 MB and does not fit on the device, so only a
few help files are here -- see |esp-docs|.  ":help" on any other subject
answers E149.  The complete reference is at https://vimhelp.org; everything
there applies here except what |esp-missing| lists.

Vim stands for Vi IMproved.  Most of Vim was made by Bram Moolenaar, but only
through the help of many others.  See |esp-credits|.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
						*esp-contents*
CONTENTS

|esp-commands|		The :Esp commands: the device itself
|esp-files|		Files, storage and the current directory
|esp-filetypes|		Which file types are recognised, and adding more
|esp-keys|		Keyboard, mouse and the terminal
|esp-busy|		The busy indicator
|esp-settings|		Settings that differ from desktop Vim
|esp-vimrc|		Your own configuration
|esp-quit|		What happens when you quit
|esp-missing|		What is not available on this device
|esp-docs|		The help files on this device
|esp-credits|		Credits and copying

==============================================================================
THE :Esp COMMANDS					*esp-commands*

Commands for the device itself.  Those that show a table open it in a small
window at the bottom: "R" refreshes it, "q" closes it.

:EspFiles [{left} [{right}]]				*:EspFiles*
		A two-pane file manager, in the style of Midnight Commander,
		in a tab page of its own.  Each pane shows a directory; both
		start in the current directory unless given.  "/" shows the
		storage roots.  The key on the left is the Midnight
		Commander one; every one has a letter too, for keyboards
		without function keys:

		<CR>  l		enter a directory, or edit a file
		<BS>  h  -	up to the parent directory
		<Tab>		switch to the other pane
		<Space>  t	tag or untag, then move down
		u		untag everything
		<F3>  v		view a file, read-only, in a new tab ("q"
				closes it)
		<F4>  e		edit a file in a new tab; ":q" comes back
		<F5>  c		copy the tagged items (or the one under the
				cursor) to the other pane's directory
		<F6>  r		move them; to the SAME directory it is a
				rename, and you are offered the name to edit
		<F7>  m		make a directory
		<F8>  d		delete, after asking; a directory goes with
				everything in it
		o		make the other pane show this directory
		<C-L>  R	refresh
		<F1>  g?	list the keys
		<F10>  q	close the file manager

		Copy and move ask for the destination, with the other pane's
		directory filled in; edit it to copy to another name.  When a
		file would be replaced you are asked, with "All" for the rest.
		CTRL-C stops a long copy.  /vimrt is read-only, and nothing
		outside /fat, /sd and /vimrt can be reached.

		Scripts can do the same with esp_fs_list(), esp_fs_copy(),
		esp_fs_move(), esp_fs_delete(), esp_fs_mkdir(), esp_fs_info()
		and esp_fs_roots().

:EspInfo						*:EspInfo*
		Chip, revision, cores, flash and PSRAM size, versions, uptime
		and why the chip last reset.

:EspHeap						*:EspHeap*
		Free memory: internal RAM, PSRAM and DMA-capable memory, with
		the largest free block and the lowest it has been since boot.
		Also how much Vim itself is using, its peak, and its budget.

:EspTasks						*:EspTasks*
		The FreeRTOS tasks, with priority, core and the least stack
		each has had free.  "vim" is this editor.

:EspGpio {pin} [{action}]				*:EspGpio*
		Read or drive a GPIO pin.  {action} is one of:
			read		show the level (the default)
			on  off		drive high or low, making the pin an
					output first if it is not one
			toggle		invert the level
			in  pullup  pulldown
					make it an input, with a pull resistor
			out  od		make it an output, push-pull or open
					drain
			reset		back to the state at power-on
		<Tab> completes pin numbers and actions.  Pins the system
		depends on -- flash, PSRAM, the console -- are refused.  Pins
		keep their state after you quit Vim, until the next reset.

:EspSerial {port} {baud} [{tx} {rx}]			*:EspSerial*
		Open serial port UART{port} (1, or 2 where the chip has it;
		UART0 is the console) and show what arrives in a window,
		live.  In that window "s" sends a line and "q" closes the
		port.  The pins default to the board's; give {tx} {rx} for
		others.
:EspSerialSend {text}					*:EspSerialSend*
		Send {text} and a line ending (g:esp_serial_eol, "\r\n" by
		default) to the open port.

:EspI2cScan [{sda} {scl}]				*:EspI2cScan*
		List the addresses of the I2C devices that answer, on the
		board's bus or the pins given.
:EspSensors [{sda} {scl}]				*:EspSensors*
		The same, with each device named where it is recognised --
		IMUs, environment sensors, touch controllers, codecs, IO
		expanders -- by its address and, where the chip has one, its
		ID register.
:EspAdc {pin}						*:EspAdc*
		Read an analog pin: the raw value and, calibrated, millivolts.

:EspNvs							*:EspNvs*
		List everything in NVS, the small key/value store that keeps
		settings across reboots.
:EspNvs {namespace}
		List one namespace.
:EspNvs {namespace} {key}
		Show one value.
:EspNvs {namespace} {key} {value}
		Store a value: digits are stored as a number, anything else
		as a string.  Namespaces and keys are at most 15 characters.
:EspNvs! {namespace} {key}
		Erase a value.
		<Tab> completes namespaces and keys.  Other parts of the
		system keep their own entries here; change those only if you
		know what they are for.

:EspReboot[!]						*:EspReboot*
		Restart the chip.  Refuses while any buffer has unsaved
		changes, unless you add !.  Unlike |esp-quit| this is a real
		reset.

NETWORK							*esp-network*

:EspNet							*:EspNet*
		The network interface: whether it is up, its address,
		gateway, DNS server and MAC address.

:EspGet[!] {url} [{file}]				*:EspGet*
		Download {url} (http:// or https://) into {file}, by default
		the last part of the URL in the current directory.  An
		existing file is only replaced with !.  HTTPS certificates are
		checked against a built-in list of public certificate
		authorities.  CTRL-C stops a download; a stopped or failed
		download never leaves a partial file behind.

The WiFi commands work on boards with WiFi, including boards where it comes from
a separate radio chip.  Elsewhere they say the build has no WiFi.

:EspWifiScan						*:EspWifiScan*
		WiFi networks in range, strongest first: name, signal,
		channel and security.

:EspWifiConnect {network} [{password}]			*:EspWifiConnect*
		Join {network}.  Without {password}, asks for it (leave it
		empty for an open network).  Waits for an address and shows
		it; CTRL-C stops waiting.  The network is remembered and
		joined again at every start.

:EspWifiDisconnect					*:EspWifiDisconnect*
		Leave the network and forget it.

:EspWifiStatus						*:EspWifiStatus*
		The same as |:EspNet|, including the network's name and
		signal strength.

:EspBleScan [{seconds}]					*:EspBleScan*
		Bluetooth LE devices in range, strongest first: name,
		address, address type, signal, and whether it accepts
		connections.  Listens for {seconds}, 5 by default, at most
		30.  Bluetooth starts the first time it is used.  Boards
		without Bluetooth say so.

Reading a web page or file works like on any Vim: >
	:e https://example.com/notes.txt
<opens it in a buffer; save a copy with ":w {file}".

Spell checking downloads its dictionary the first time you use it: >
	:set spell spelllang=en
<asks whether to fetch the English dictionary and saves it in
/fat/.vim/spell/.  It needs the network, and space: English is about 1 MB.

BLUETOOTH KEYBOARDS					*esp-bt-keyboard*

On boards with Bluetooth, a Bluetooth LE keyboard can be the keyboard: its keys
go to Vim just as the console's do, and both work at once.

:EspBtKeyboard						*:EspBtKeyboard*
		The keyboard: connected or not, its name and address, battery
		level, and the last key report received.

:EspBtKeyboard scan [{seconds}]
		Put the keyboard in pairing mode first (see its manual; often a
		long press on a Bluetooth key).  Lists the keyboards in range,
		numbered.  Many keyboards use a new address each time they enter
		pairing mode, so pair straight after scanning.

:EspBtKeyboard pair {n}
:EspBtKeyboard pair {address} [random]
		Connect to keyboard {n} from the list (or at {address}) and
		pair with it.  No passkey is asked for: running this command is
		the confirmation.  After that the keyboard comes back by itself,
		also after a restart: press a key to wake it.

:EspBtKeyboard forget
		Disconnect the keyboard and forget it.


REMOTE FILES: SCP AND SFTP				*esp-ssh*

Files on other machines are reached over SSH, with Vim's usual URLs: >
	:e scp://me@server/notes.txt		relative to your home there
	:e scp://me@server//etc/hosts		an absolute path
	:w sftp://me@server:2222/copy.txt	another port
	:EspFiles /fat sftp://me@server/	a remote pane in the file manager
<In the file manager a remote pane can be browsed and changed like a local
one: copy and move between it and a local pane (directories included),
make directories, delete, rename.  Copying between two remote hosts is not
supported.

:EspSshKeygen[!]					*:EspSshKeygen*
		Make the device's SSH key, /fat/.ssh/id_ecdsa (ECDSA P-256),
		and show the public half: add that line to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
		on each server.  With an existing key, shows it; ! replaces it.
		Without a key, the device can still log in with a password,
		which it asks for and remembers until Vim restarts.

The first time you connect to a server you are shown its key fingerprint
and asked whether to trust it; the answer is kept in /fat/.ssh/known_hosts.
If a server's key later changes the connection is refused -- that is what
a man-in-the-middle looks like.  If you know why it changed, delete that
server's line from /fat/.ssh/known_hosts.

THE WEB INTERFACE					*esp-web*

A browser on the same network can manage the device's files, change editor
settings and watch what is being edited -- line, column, word and character
counts -- live.  It is off until you start it, and it needs a password.

:EspWebPasswd						*:EspWebPasswd*
		Set the web password (at least 8 characters).  There is no
		default password; the web interface will not start without
		one.  Only a salted hash is stored.  Setting it logs every
		browser out.

:EspWebStart [{port}]					*:EspWebStart*
		Start the web interface (port 443 unless given) and show its
		address and the SHA-256 fingerprint of its certificate.

:EspWebStop						*:EspWebStop*
		Stop it; every browser is logged out.

:EspWebStatus						*:EspWebStatus*
		Whether it is running, its address, how many browsers are
		logged in, and the certificate fingerprint.

The connection is HTTPS with a certificate the device made itself, so the
browser warns that it does not know it.  Compare the fingerprint the browser
shows with the one :EspWebStart printed; if they match, the connection is to
this device.  The certificate is kept, so this is needed once per browser.

Files: browse, download, upload (also by dropping files on the page), make
folders, rename and delete -- with the same checks as |:EspFiles|: /vimrt is
read-only and nothing outside /fat, /sd and /vimrt can be reached.

Settings: tabstop, shiftwidth, expandtab, number, relativenumber, wrap,
background and colorscheme.  Saved on the device and applied within a
second, and again at every start.

Security: logins are refused for a while after wrong passwords, at most four
browsers are logged in at once, a session ends after an hour unused, and
every change needs a token only the logged-in page has.

The same information is available to scripts, as Dicts and Lists:
esp_info(), esp_heap(), esp_tasks(), esp_gpio_pins(), esp_gpio_read(),
esp_gpio_write(), esp_gpio_mode(), esp_nvs_list(), esp_nvs_get(),
esp_nvs_set(), esp_nvs_erase(), esp_reboot(), esp_net_status(),
esp_http_get(), and for SSH esp_ssh_get(), esp_ssh_put(), esp_ssh_list(),
esp_ssh_mkdir(), esp_ssh_remove(), esp_ssh_rename(), esp_ssh_hostkey(),
esp_ssh_trust(), esp_ssh_keygen(), and for the web interface esp_web_start(),
esp_web_stop(), esp_web_info(), esp_web_passwd(), esp_settings(), and for the
hardware esp_serial_open(), esp_serial_read(), esp_serial_write(),
esp_serial_close(), esp_i2c_scan(), esp_sensors(), esp_adc_read().  Try ":echo esp_heap()".
esp_gpio_read() returns -1, with an error, for a pin it may not use.

==============================================================================
FILES AND STORAGE					*esp-files*

The device has two filesystems:

	/fat		Your files.  Flash storage, kept across reboots.
			This is $HOME, and Vim starts with /fat as the
			current directory.
	/vimrt		Vim's runtime files ($VIMRUNTIME): syntax files,
			plugins, this help.  Read-only.

Everything you write goes under /fat.  Relative names work as usual: ":w
notes.txt" writes /fat/notes.txt, ":cd" changes directory, ":pwd" shows it,
and <Tab> completes file names on the command line (":e no<Tab>").

To manage files use |:EspFiles|, a two-pane file manager.  To browse a
single directory use ":Explore" (or ":e /fat").  Move to a name and press
<Enter> to open it, "-" to go up a directory.  This is Vim's netrw plugin, for
local files; its full manual is |netrw|, and |esp-missing| lists the network
features that do not work here.

Flash is small (a few megabytes) and wears with writing, which is why swap,
backup and undo files are off by default -- see |esp-settings|.

==============================================================================
FILE TYPES						*esp-filetypes*

Vim recognises these file types by name (nearly all with syntax highlighting):

@FILETYPES@

A file with no matching name is still recognised from a first line such as
"#!/bin/sh".  Other file types are deliberately not detected, which keeps
memory use low.  Check what Vim decided with ":set filetype?".

To add a file type on the device, without rebuilding, put an ftdetect script
in /fat/.vim/ftdetect/.  For example /fat/.vim/ftdetect/foo.vim: >
	au BufNewFile,BufRead *.foo  setf foo
<Its syntax file then goes in /fat/.vim/syntax/foo.vim.  (The built-in list
comes from esp-vim/filetypes.conf in the firmware sources.)

==============================================================================
KEYBOARD, MOUSE AND THE TERMINAL			*esp-keys*

Interrupt		CTRL-C stops a long command, a runaway search or a
			loop, and returns to Normal mode.  Wait a moment
			before typing again: Vim discards keys that were
			already queued when it was interrupted.

Backspace		Works whether your terminal sends DEL or CTRL-H.

Keyboards		Besides the terminal, a Bluetooth keyboard can type into
			Vim on boards with Bluetooth; see |esp-bt-keyboard|.
			It has the keys of a US PC keyboard; F1-F12, the cursor
			keys, Home, End, Page Up/Down, Insert and Delete send
			what an xterm sends.

Mouse			Enabled, using SGR mouse reporting.  Click to move
			the cursor, drag to select, wheel to scroll.

Screen size		Measured from your terminal when Vim starts.  If you
			resize the terminal window afterwards, tell Vim: >
				:set lines=40 columns=120
<			Without an answer from the terminal (a log capture, a
			basic serial monitor) the size is 24 lines by 80
			columns.

Connecting		Vim talks to a terminal over the serial console.  Use
			a terminal program that passes keys through
			unchanged ("raw" mode): arrow keys arriving as text
			such as "^[[A" mean the terminal is not in raw mode.

==============================================================================
THE BUSY INDICATOR					*esp-busy*

Some things take a noticeable moment on a microcontroller: opening a large
file, the first file of a new type (its syntax rules are loaded), ":help".
When Vim has been working for more than about a third of a second, a small
spinner turns at the bottom right of the screen.  It disappears as soon
as Vim is ready for your next key.  CTRL-C interrupts the work.

To turn it off: >
	:let g:esp_busy = 0
<
==============================================================================
SETTINGS THAT DIFFER FROM DESKTOP VIM			*esp-settings*

These are set by the system vimrc, /vimrt/vimrc:

	'noswapfile'	No swap files: they would be written to flash
			continuously.  Save often with ":w".
	'nobackup' 'nowritebackup'
			No backup copies.
	'noundofile'	Undo history is kept while Vim runs, not across
			restarts.
	'viminfo' empty	Command history, marks and registers are not saved
			between sessions.
	'nomodeline'	Files arrive over the network, so they are not
			allowed to change settings when opened.
	'regexpengine' 1
			The backtracking engine; it uses far less memory
			here.  Syntax highlighting gives up on a pattern
			that takes longer than 'redrawtime'; a slow search
			you started yourself is stopped with CTRL-C.
	'encoding' utf-8

To keep undo history across restarts, for example: >
	:call mkdir('/fat/.vim/undo', 'p')
	:set undofile undodir=/fat/.vim/undo
<Put those lines in your vimrc (|esp-vimrc|) to make them permanent.

==============================================================================
YOUR OWN CONFIGURATION					*esp-vimrc*

Put your settings in /fat/.vimrc.  Note one thing Vim does everywhere, which
is easy to miss: when a personal vimrc exists, defaults.vim is no longer
loaded -- and it is what turns on syntax highlighting, file type plugins and
the mouse.  Start /fat/.vimrc with this line to keep them: >
	source $VIMRUNTIME/defaults.vim
<Plugins and scripts go under /fat/.vim/ as on any Unix: plugin/, syntax/,
ftplugin/, colors/, ftdetect/, and doc/ for their help (run ":helptags
/fat/.vim/doc" after adding help files).

==============================================================================
QUITTING						*esp-quit*

Vim is the only thing running on the device, so there is nowhere to "exit" to.
When the last window closes -- ":q", ":qa!", ":wq", "ZZ" -- you see: >
	All buffers closed.  Vim is forever.
	Press any key to start a new session.
<and the next key starts Vim again, exactly as at power-on: no buffers, no
variables or options left over from before, the current directory back at
/fat.  This takes a moment, not a reboot.  Files you saved are of course still
there; changes you quit without saving are gone, as always.

==============================================================================
NOT AVAILABLE ON THIS DEVICE				*esp-missing*

There is no operating system shell and no other programs, so everything that
runs an external command is unavailable:

	:!cmd  :r !cmd  :w !cmd  system()  filters (!{motion})
				No shell.  Vim reports E371.
	:terminal  job_start()	No processes to run.
	:make			Needs a compiler.  ":vimgrep" works.
	:grep			Uses an external program.  Use ":vimgrep".

Also not available:

	This documentation	Only the files in |esp-docs|.  E149 for other
				subjects; see https://vimhelp.org.
	System clipboard	The "* and "+ registers are not connected to
				anything.  Other registers work normally.
	CTRL-X CTRL-O		Omni-completion engines are not installed.
				CTRL-N, CTRL-P and CTRL-X CTRL-F work.
	{a,b} and `cmd`		Brace and backtick expansion in file names.
	Network access		netrw's ftp://, rsync:// and other methods that
				run programs.  http://, https://, scp:// and
				sftp:// work (|esp-network|, |esp-ssh|).
	Remote directories	":e scp://host/dir/" cannot list a directory;
				use |:EspFiles| for remote browsing.

==============================================================================
HELP FILES ON THIS DEVICE				*esp-docs*

	|help.txt|	This file.
	|netrw.txt|	The file browser, ":Explore".
	|version9.txt|	What is new in Vim 9 (":help version9").  The list of
			individual patches is left out to save flash.
	|uganda.txt|	Vim's license, and the charity it asks you to support.
	|sponsor.txt|	Sponsoring Vim development.

Links inside those files that lead to Vim's other documentation are shown as
plain text, since that documentation is not here.

==============================================================================
CREDITS AND COPYING					*esp-credits*

Vim is written by Bram Moolenaar and the Vim project, with contributions from
many others.  It is distributed under the Vim license, which lets you use and
copy it freely; if you find it useful, consider supporting the charity it asks
for, the ICCF (https://iccf-holland.org).  The license is |uganda.txt|, and
|sponsor| explains how to support Vim development.

This help file is an amended version of Vim's help.txt, rewritten for this
device.  The rest of Vim here is unmodified apart from a few small patches
needed to build it for the @CHIP@.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*bars*		Bars example

Now that you've jumped here with CTRL-], g<LeftMouse>, or <C-LeftMouse>, you
can use CTRL-T, CTRL-O, g<RightMouse>, or <C-RightMouse> to go back to where
you were.

Note that tags are within | characters, but when highlighting is enabled these
characters are hidden.  That makes it easier to read a command.

Anyway, you can use CTRL-] on any word, also when it is not within |, and Vim
will try to find help for it.  Especially for options in single quotes, e.g.
'hlsearch'.

